                              Turbo Newscan
                            by Mack Lifeguard
                            68000 code by NZO

                         ** Dual Crew-Shining **

Hello, and welcome to the fastest newscan replacement door you've ever
seen on Amiexpress.  There has never been anything like this before.  You
just watch, this tool will revolutionize the Newscan Door industry.

If you are familiar with Turbo Zippy, then you will love this adaptation
of it into the newscan "n" command of /X.

Only, Turbo Newscan (TN) is roughly 2.3 times faster than Turbo Zippy!

I won't go into details and make charts and stuff like I did last time for
Turbo Zippy, but lets just say that TN can do a newscan of a 2 MEG
directory listing in about 2 (two) seconds on my 7Mhz A500.  Amiexpress
does it in about 82 seconds.  So TN is about 40 times faster.

If you have an accelerated Amiga, you will notice the results
instantaneously.  You have to see it to believe me!

The only catch is that you have enough memory. If you have a multi-node
board with large directory listings, I recommend at least 10 Megs of free
ram. This tool is for elites only. You want the ultimate speed and power?
Then it will cost you ram chips. But hey, look at the IBM world, they need
16 Megs just to run their new OS!


                            FEATURES OVERVIEW
                            -----------------
Turbo Newscan:
*40 times faster than Amiexpress and Byteandi/Mystic Newscan.
*Doesn't choke when it encounters colors in the listings (Byteandi's does).
*Doesn't choke when it encounters non-standard NUKE banners and such. ^^^^
*Choose from 9 different date modes, (today, yesterday, -x, weekly, etc.)
*Compatible with all /X releases from /X 2.39 and up. And of course the
 cool new Sigma Express!
*Coded in super-fast assembler for best XIM performance.
*True arrow key selecting of files. Unlike other newscan doors, you can use
 both the keypad AND your arrow keys to highlight files. Plus the cursor
 wraps around to the next file. None of these features are available in the
 other newscanner doors.



                               INSTALLATION
                               ------------
The TN executable is an XIM door, so simply add it to your CustomCommands
file. Something like this:

Step 1.

n        XM010BBS:Doors/TurboN
                   ^
                   |
                   |__ or wherever you keep your doors.

Notice there is no '*' before the 'n'. Don't put one there, or Turbo
Newscan will not work properly. If you are one of the unfortunate few to
be running the appalling 3.x version of Amiexpress, then you will have to
fumble about with those nasty icons and other tedious files. Turbo Newscan
will work perfectly on 3.x, but it's a bitch to install it.  Don't blame
me!  I think the new icon method sucks!


Step 2.

Copy the included TN.HELP file to your S: directory.

If you are using a version of Amiexpress *below* 2.39, then the [s]
last-logon date will be incorrect. This is a bug that was fixed in the
later versions of /X.

                                That's it!

                   Important Note About Cursor Flagging
                   =-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-

        (It's a good idea to capture this section and send an EALL)

The new [f]lag mode of Turbo Newscan makes an ANSI bitmap of the display
screen.  In order for this to work properly you will need to check 2
things:

1. You are in ansi mode.
2. You have selected the correct /X screen length.

If you are getting weird results when using the [f]lag mode of TN, then
chances are that you have not set your screen length properly. Simply go to
the /X prompt and hit 'w' followed by '5'.

Sysops:  If you are watching a user while he is flagging files, your end
will look like a mess if you have a different screen setting. This is
because location #0,1 on your screen may be different than the remote.  But
don't be alarmed!  The user's end looks just fine.  Or adjust your settings
and resolution to match the average length of 23 lines.


